<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/867">867</a>–933</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Li Siyuan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang, Li Siyuan, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> general and adopted son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> who took the throne in 926 after the death of Emperor Zhuangzong. His reign restored a measure of order to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and under his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-dao">Feng Dao</a> work began in 932 on the first printed edition of the Confucian classics. His son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a> founded Later Jin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 清善成堂刻本 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%94%90%E6%98%8E%E5%AE%97.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
